    "answer": {
      "short": "The denominator becomes $(\\sqrt{3}-1)(\\sqrt{3}+1)=2$, which is rational.\n\nThe difference of two squares works with the negative of the strict conjugate too.",
      "worked": "The strict conjugate of $\\sqrt{3}-1$ is $-\\sqrt{3}-1$. Its negative, $\\sqrt{3}+1$, also gives a difference of squares:\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\sqrt{3}-1)(\\sqrt{3}+1)&=(\\sqrt{3})^2-1^2\\\\(\\sqrt{3}-1)(\\sqrt{3}+1)&=2\\end{align*}$$\nHence the fraction becomes $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{2}$, with a rational denominator.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
